WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.112 --> 00:00:02.070
SPEAKER: For data
scientists, Jupyter notebooks

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:05.493
is a de facto tool for
performing rapid data analysis.

00:00:05.493 --> 00:00:07.410
And you might have seen
in our previous videos

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.780
that Kubeflow deployments
include services for spotting

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:11.258
and managing Jupyter notebooks.

00:00:11.258 --> 00:00:13.050
Let's take a look at
what a notebook can do

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:13.967
and how to set one up.

00:00:13.967 --> 00:00:17.251
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.103 --> 00:00:20.270
If you want to check out
the documentation directly,

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:22.520
check out the link below to
read more about setting up

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:23.610
notebooks on Kubeflow.

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:25.380
Notebooks on Kubeflow
are a great place

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:26.610
to start with Kubeflow.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:29.550
You can take a smaller data set,
train it, and serve your model

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:30.780
using notebooks.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:32.877
While you could run a
Jupyter notebook locally,

00:00:32.877 --> 00:00:35.460
there are some amazing benefits
that Kubeflow-hosted notebooks

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:38.070
give you, namely, you
have better integration

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:39.790
with other Kubeflow components.

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:41.580
In other words
notebooks integrate well

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.497
with the rest of the
infrastructure for things

00:00:43.497 --> 00:00:46.140
like accessing other services
in the Kubeflow cluster

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.330
via their cluster IP addresses.

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:51.720
They also integrate with access
control and authentication.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:54.060
Administrators can set up
standard role-based access

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.190
for the entire
organization, providing

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.740
consistent and
centralized security.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:00.480
Secondly, it's easy
to share notebooks

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.480
across the organization and
configure who can access them

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:06.840
because you can deploy them on
pods in a Kubernetes cluster.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.240
Plus, a lot of common
libraries come preloaded

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:10.770
in the default notebook image.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.750
For example, it
includes all the plugins

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.420
that you need to train a
TensorFlow model with Jupyter,

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:18.510
including TensorBoard for rich
visualizations and insights

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:19.840
into your model.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:22.680
And lastly, you can leverage
the power of customized compute

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:24.720
shapes and GPUs for
machine learning

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.760
that's compute intensive.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.130
With notebooks on Kubeflow,
you're hosting your notebook

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:30.840
on a Kubernetes pod.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.550
You can setup as
many notebook servers

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:35.220
as you like in each
Kubeflow deployment.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:37.680
Each notebook server can
have multiple notebooks.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.770
And each notebook belongs
to one Kubernetes namespace,

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:43.020
which matches a
project or a team name.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.150
A user can then
configure who can access

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:46.650
their individual namespace.

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:49.350
Let's walk through a setup of
a Jupyter notebook on Kubeflow.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.510
In the Kubeflow UI,
click Notebook Servers

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.183
in the left-hand panel.

00:01:53.183 --> 00:01:54.600
Choose the namespace
corresponding

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:56.310
to your Kubeflow profile.

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:58.973
Click New Server to
create a notebook server.

00:01:58.973 --> 00:02:00.390
You should see a
page for entering

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:02.630
details of your new server.

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:05.390
Enter a name of your choice
for the notebook server.

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:08.009
Select a Docker image for
the baseline deployment

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:09.330
of your notebook server.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.700
You can choose from a
range of standard images

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:13.830
or specify a custom image.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:16.380
You can keep the
default CPU allocation.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:20.435
But for CPU-intensive jobs, you
can choose more than one CPU.

00:02:20.435 --> 00:02:22.560
Choose the total amount of
memory for your notebook

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:23.890
server.

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:25.855
Finally, by default,
Kubeflow provisions

00:02:25.855 --> 00:02:28.710
a Kubernetes
persistent volume, PV,

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:31.470
for each of the your data
volumes with 10 gigabytes.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:34.380
But you can also configure
your own and specify it here.

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.990
This is to hold your personal
workspace for this notebook

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:37.830
server.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.960
That way, you can
retain data even if you

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:41.220
destroy your notebook server.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:42.503
Click Launch.

00:02:42.503 --> 00:02:44.670
You should see an entry for
your new notebook server

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:47.340
on the Notebook Servers page
with a spinning indicator

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:48.810
in the Status column.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:51.270
It can take a few minutes to
set up the notebook server.

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.110
When the server
provisioning is complete,

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:54.750
you should see an
entry for your server

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.510
on the Notebook Servers page
with a checkmark in the Status

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:58.530
column.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:01.890
Next, you can click Connect
to start the notebook server.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:04.320
Click Upload to upload
an existing notebook.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:07.650
But let's go ahead and create
an empty Python 3 notebook.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:09.630
To test our Jupyter
installation,

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:13.260
we'll run a basic "Hello World"
program, a very simple MNIST

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.630
classifier which uses
image recognition

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:17.970
to classify handwritten digits.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:22.800
And we get an accuracy
of 0.9016 back.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:24.840
There you have it,
notebooks on Kubeflow.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:26.340
It gives you the
same experience you

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:29.100
love about Jupyter notebooks
with the added benefits

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:31.770
of image extensibility,
scalability,

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:34.830
access control, sharing and
submitting jobs directly

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:36.690
on your Kubernetes cluster.

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:38.880
It's the easiest way for
you to immediately start

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:40.530
experimenting on Kubeflow.

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:41.910
You can read more
about notebooks

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:43.650
on Kubeflow in the link below.

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:45.660
And stay tuned for
the next episode.

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:49.010
[MUSIC PLAYING]

